# CasperProver — Red-Team Self-Audit

Methodology: each row checked against actual source. Verdicts: **RESIST** = mitigated in code
today. **PARTIAL** = mitigated with a known/documented gap. **OPEN** = needs a fix.

| # | Attack Vector | Category | Result | Evidence / Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Double-slash the same proof | Contract | RESIST | `SLASHED_DICT` marks `proof_id` once slashed, `report_and_slash` reverts `ERR_ALREADY_SLASHED` on repeat |
| 2 | U512 overflow/underflow in staking | Contract | RESIST | `record_stake`/`unstake` use `checked_add`/`checked_sub`, revert `ERR_INSUFFICIENT_STAKE` rather than underflow |
| 3 | Unauthorized proof revocation | Contract | RESIST | `revoke_proof` in `proof-registry` checks `caller == submitter`, reverts `ERR_UNAUTHORIZED` |
| 4 | Duplicate agent registration | Contract | RESIST | `register_agent` reverts `ERR_AGENT_EXISTS` if the caller already has a record |
| 5 | Unbounded input DoS (oversized strings in named args) | Contract | RESIST | `ERR_INPUT_TOO_LONG` guard present on proof submission and agent registration |
| 6 | `report_and_slash` called by a non-owner to steal the bounty | Contract | RESIST (by design) | Entry point is intentionally permissionless — comment in code confirms this is a whistleblower-bounty pattern; it only pays out once `proof-registry` state shows `revoked == 1` and `agent` matches the proof's own recorded agent, so a random caller can only trigger a payout that was already earned, not create one |
| 7 | Fake/invalid ZK proof accepted | Backend | RESIST | Groth16 BN254 verifier (gnark) rejects invalid proofs — not just a format check |
| 8 | PQ signature forgery (ML-DSA-65 / Lamport-OTS) | Backend | RESIST | Verification path checked, no accept-on-parse-failure fallback found |
| 9 | Verifier-gate spam / rate abuse on `batch_check` | Contract | RESIST | `ERR_BATCH_TOO_LARGE` cap present; `verify`/`is_valid` also gated by `ERR_RATE_LIMIT` in `verifier-gate` |
| 10 | API auth bypass on mutating endpoints | Backend | PARTIAL | `authMiddleware` requires `X-API-Key` on all non-GET/HEAD requests **only if `API_KEY` env var is set**; if unset, every request passes through unauthenticated. Code comment documents this as an intentional demo default (`KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md`) — verify `API_KEY` is actually set on the Render production deployment before submission, since an unset var silently degrades to "no auth" rather than failing loud |
| 11 | Rate-limit bypass under judge/demo load | Backend | PARTIAL | In-memory per-IP limiter (60 req/min, `rateLimiter` struct) — resets on process restart, bypassable via IP rotation, same class of gap as AE402's limiter |
| 12 | Panic-based crash on malformed input | Contract | RESIST | 0 raw `.unwrap()`/`panic!` found in the 8 contracts — all fallible calls go through `unwrap_or_revert()` |
| 13 | Panic-based crash in Go engine | Backend | RESIST | 0 `panic(` calls found in `engine/internal` |
| 14 | Secrets committed to repo | Backend/CI | RESIST | TruffleHog secret-scan on push+PR; Dependabot active for Go/npm/Cargo/Actions |
| 15 | CORS misconfiguration | Backend | RESIST | `corsMiddleware` sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` but never sets `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials`, so browsers won't attach cookies/credentials to cross-origin requests — same safe pattern as AE402 |

## Summary
0 production-code panics across 8 contracts and the Go engine — strongest result of the three projects reviewed. #10 and #11 are real but documented, low-severity gaps (demo-scale auth/rate-limiting, not fund-safety).
